course: dictionary
lesson: 14696
title: Words that mean more than they say
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Take the word priceless. «The museum keeps a priceless painting that no one could ever buy.» Here priceless means worth so much that no price could be put on it.
explain: Now read this: «Your face when the clown tripped was priceless!» A face cannot cost money, so priceless here cannot mean worth a fortune. In everyday talk it means very funny. Ask yourself: what would fit in this sentence? Then try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Priceless is also used with words like memories: «Those photos from our holiday are priceless to me.» You know the photos are not for sale, so the sentence is about how much they mean to you. The words around the hard word are the clues.
fact: Priceless means worth so much that no price could be put on it.
fact: In everyday talk, «priceless» can mean very funny: «That joke was priceless!»
fact: Phrases you might meet are a priceless painting and priceless memories.
fact: Some words have more than one meaning, so check the words around them.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «The museum keeps a priceless painting.» What does priceless mean here? | Broken and old | Very funny | *Worth so much that no price could be put on it | Cheap and easy to find
exercise: «Your face when the clown tripped was priceless!» What does priceless mean here? | *Very funny | Hard to see | Made of gold | Worth a lot of money
exercise: Which phrase is about memories that mean more than money can buy? | cheap paper | *priceless memories | empty box | broken clock
test: «Those letters from my gran are priceless to me.» What does priceless suggest? | *They mean so much to me that money could never pay for them | They are very funny letters | They were posted late | They are cheap, so I can throw them away
test: Ben says, «That cartoon was priceless!» What does Ben mean? | *It was very funny | It was made of metal | It was too quiet to hear | It was worth a lot of money
exam: Which meaning fits best in «The painting was priceless, so the museum put a guard beside it»? | Very hungry | Very funny | Short and rude | *Worth so much that no price could be put on it
